.TH MINERD 1 "September 2026" "cpuminer-multi 1.1.0"
.SH NAME
minerd \- multi-algorithm CPU miner
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B minerd
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B minerd
is a multi-threaded CPU miner for Bitcoin, Litecoin and other
cryptocurrencies, with many proof-of-work algorithms (see \fB\-a\fR).
It mines for a pool over the Stratum protocol, or solo against a coin's own
node over the getblocktemplate protocol (BIP 22 and 23).
It also speaks getwork, the protocol getblocktemplate replaced, and the
JSON-RPC 2.0 protocol of Monero (RandomX) and CryptoNight pools.
.PP
In its normal mode of operation, \fBminerd\fR connects to a mining server
(specified with the \fB\-o\fR option), receives work from it and starts hashing.
As soon as a solution is found, it is submitted to the same mining server,
which can accept or reject it.
Over HTTP, \fBminerd\fR takes advantage of long polling if the server
supports it; in any case, fresh work is fetched as needed.
With Stratum the server is responsible for sending fresh work at least
every two minutes; if it fails to do so, \fBminerd\fR stops hashing until it
does, and reconnects if the connection is lost.
JSON-RPC 2.0 pools can go longer without a new job: after a minute of
silence \fBminerd\fR sends a keep-alive, and it reconnects after five.
.PP
When solo mining with getblocktemplate, \fBminerd\fR builds the coinbase
transaction itself, paying the block reward to the address given with
\fB\-\-coinbase\-addr\fR, with the block height (BIP 34) and, where segwit
is active, the witness commitment (BIP 141).
It asks the node (validateaddress) which output script pays to that address.
A node that requires a rule \fBminerd\fR does not implement
(such as Litecoin's MWEB) is refused with a message.
The node of a coin with several algorithms (DigiByte) is asked for a
template for the algorithm being mined.
.PP
By default, \fBminerd\fR writes all its messages to standard error.
On systems that have a syslog, the \fB\-\-syslog\fR option can be used
to write to it instead.
.PP
On start, the nice value of all miner threads is set to 19.
On Linux, the scheduling policy is also changed to SCHED_IDLE,
or to SCHED_BATCH if that fails.
On multiprocessor systems, \fBminerd\fR
automatically sets the CPU affinity of miner threads
if the number of threads is a multiple of the number of processors.
.SH EXAMPLES
To connect to a Litecoin mining pool that provides a Stratum server
at example.com on port 3333, authenticating as worker "foo" with password "bar":
.PP
.nf
.RS
minerd \-o stratum+tcp://example.com:3333 \-O foo:bar
.RE
.fi
.PP
To mine solo on a local Bitcoin testnet node whose RPC server listens on
port 18332, with RPC user name "rpcuser" and password "rpcpass",
paying the reward to a testnet address:
.PP
.nf
.RS
minerd \-a sha256d \-o http://localhost:18332 \-O rpcuser:rpcpass \e
    \-\-coinbase\-addr=tb1qw508d6qejxtdg4y5r3zarvary0c5xw7kxpjzsx
.RE
.fi
.PP
To mine Monero on a local P2Pool node, in the background, sending the
output to the syslog facility and omitting the per-thread hashmeter output:
.PP
.nf
.RS
minerd \-BSq \-a randomx \-o stratum+tcp://127.0.0.1:3333 \-u x \-p x
.RE
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-algo\fR=\fIALGORITHM\fR
Set the hashing algorithm to use.
Default is scrypt.
Possible values are:
.RS 11
.TP 13
.B scrypt
scrypt(1024, 1, 1) (Litecoin, Dogecoin)
.TP
.B scrypt:\fIN\fR
scrypt(\fIN\fR, 1, 1) (\fIN\fR must be a power of 2 greater than 1)
.TP
.B sha256d
SHA-256d (Bitcoin and many others)
.TP
.B x11
X11 (Dash)
.TP
.B blake
BLAKE-256 with 8 rounds (Blakecoin, and the coins merge-mined with it)
.TP
.B skein
SHA-256 of Skein-512 (DigiByte)
.TP
.B qubit
Qubit (DigiByte)
.TP
.B odo
Odocrypt (DigiByte); \fBodocrypt\fR is accepted too.
Over Stratum, the key is derived from the block time with the main network's
10-day period; solo mining uses the key the node gives.
.TP
.B neoscrypt
NeoScrypt(128, 2, 1) with Salsa20/20 and ChaCha20/20 (Feathercoin)
.TP
.B argon2d4096
Argon2d 1.3 with 4 MiB, 1 pass, 1 lane (Myriad, Unitus)
.TP
.B argon2d500
Argon2d 1.0 with 500 KiB, 2 passes, 8 lanes (Dynamic)
.TP
.B argon2d250
Argon2d 1.0 with 250 KiB, 1 pass, 4 lanes (Credits)
.TP
.B argon2d16000
Argon2d 1.0 with 16000 KiB, 1 pass, 1 lane (Alterdot)
.TP
.B yescrypt
yescrypt 0.5, N=2048, r=8, the block header as key (Myriad, GlobalBoost-Y)
.TP
.B yescryptr8
yescrypt 0.5, N=2048, r=8, key "Client Key"
.TP
.B yescryptr16
yescrypt 0.5, N=4096, r=16, key "Client Key"
.TP
.B yescryptr32
yescrypt 0.5, N=4096, r=32, key "WaviBanana"
.TP
.B yespower
yespower 1.0, N=2048, r=32, no key, unless \fB\-\-param\-n\fR,
\fB\-\-param\-r\fR or \fB\-\-param\-key\fR say otherwise
.TP
.B yespowerr16
yespower 1.0, N=4096, r=16, no key (Yenten)
.TP
.B randomx
RandomX, the rx/0 variant (Monero); \fBrx/0\fR is accepted too.
See \fB\-\-randomx\-mode\fR.
.TP
.B verthash
Verthash (Vertcoin); see \fB\-\-verthash\-data\fR.
.TP
.B cryptonight
CryptoNight, the original version (Bytecoin)
.TP
.B keccak
Keccak-256 (Maxcoin)
.TP
.B quark
Quark (Quarkcoin)
.TP
.B shavite3
SHAvite-3 (INKcoin)
.TP
.B fresh
Fresh (FreshCoin)
.TP
.B x13
X13
.TP
.B x14
X14
.TP
.B x15
X15
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-benchmark\fR
Run in offline benchmark mode.
.TP
\fB\-B\fR, \fB\-\-background\fR
Run in the background as a daemon.
.TP
\fB\-\-cert\fR=\fIFILE\fR
Set an SSL certificate to use with the mining server.
Only supported when using the HTTPS protocol.
.TP
\fB\-\-coinbase\-addr\fR=\fIADDRESS\fR
Solo mining (getblocktemplate): the address to pay the block reward to.
Any address type the node knows can be used; for nodes that cannot say
which output script an address pays to, legacy (base58), segwit (bech32)
and taproot (bech32m) addresses are decoded by \fBminerd\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-coinbase\-sig\fR=\fITEXT\fR
Solo mining (getblocktemplate): text to include in the coinbase of the
blocks found.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR=\fIFILE\fR
Load options from a configuration file.
\fIFILE\fR must contain a JSON object
mapping long options to their arguments (as strings),
or to \fBtrue\fR if no argument is required.
Sample configuration file:

.nf
	{
		"url": "stratum+tcp://example.com:3333",
		"userpass": "foo:bar",
		"retry-pause": "10",
		"quiet": true
	}
.fi
.TP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-debug\fR
Enable debug output.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-diff\-factor\fR=\fIN\fR
Divide the share difficulty the pool sets by \fIN\fR,
for pools that express difficulty relative to another "difficulty 1"
than \fBminerd\fR. Default is 1.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a help message and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-gbt\fR
Do not use getblocktemplate (use getwork).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-getwork\fR
Do not fall back to getwork when the server does not support
getblocktemplate.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-longpoll\fR
Do not use long polling.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-redirect\fR
Ignore requests from the server to switch to a different URL.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-stratum\fR
Do not switch to Stratum, even if the server advertises support for it.
.TP
\fB\-\-param\-n\fR=\fIN\fR, \fB\-\-param\-r\fR=\fIR\fR, \fB\-\-param\-key\fR=\fITEXT\fR
yescrypt and yespower: the N (a power of 2 from 1024 to 524288), r (8
to 32) and personalization string of the coin, where they differ from the
algorithm's.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-url\fR=[\fISCHEME\fR://][\fIUSERNAME\fR[:\fIPASSWORD\fR]@]\fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR[/\fIPATH\fR]
Set the URL of the mining server to connect to.
Supported schemes are \fBhttp\fR, \fBhttps\fR and \fBstratum+tcp\fR.
If no scheme is specified, http is assumed.
Specifying a \fIPATH\fR is only supported for HTTP and HTTPS.
Specifying credentials has the same effect as using the \fB\-O\fR option.
.TP
\fB\-O\fR, \fB\-\-userpass\fR=\fIUSERNAME\fR:\fIPASSWORD\fR
Set the credentials to use for connecting to the mining server.
Any value previously set with \fB\-u\fR or \fB\-p\fR is discarded.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-pass\fR=\fIPASSWORD\fR
Set the password to use for connecting to the mining server.
Any password previously set with \fB\-O\fR is discarded.
.TP
\fB\-P\fR, \fB\-\-protocol\-dump\fR
Enable output of all protocol-level activities.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Disable per-thread hashmeter output.
.TP
\fB\-\-randomx\-mode\fR=\fIMODE\fR
RandomX: \fBfast\fR, with a 2080 MiB dataset shared by the threads, built
from a 256 MiB cache when mining starts and whenever the pool's seed hash
changes (every 2048 blocks on Monero); \fBlight\fR, with the cache only,
several times slower; or \fBauto\fR, the default: fast mode if the machine,
or the container it runs in, has the memory, and the program is 64-bit.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-retries\fR=\fIN\fR
Set the maximum number of times to retry if a network call fails.
If not specified, the miner will retry indefinitely.
.TP
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-retry\-pause\fR=\fISECONDS\fR
Set how long to wait between retries. Default is 10 seconds.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-scantime\fR=\fISECONDS\fR
Set an upper bound on the time the miner can go without fetching fresh work.
This setting has no effect in Stratum mode or when long polling is activated.
Default is 5 seconds.
.TP
\fB\-S\fR, \fB\-\-syslog\fR
Log to the syslog facility instead of standard error.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-threads\fR=\fIN\fR
Set the number of miner threads.
If not specified, the miner will try to detect the number of available processors
and use that; for \fBrandomx\fR and \fBcryptonight\fR, no more threads than
the processor's L3 cache holds 2 MiB scratchpads.
.TP
\fB\-T\fR, \fB\-\-timeout\fR=\fISECONDS\fR
Set a timeout for long polling.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-user\fR=\fIUSERNAME\fR
Set the username to use for connecting to the mining server.
Any username previously set with \fB\-O\fR is discarded.
.TP
\fB\-\-verthash\-data\fR=\fIFILE\fR
Verthash: the data file (1.2 GB), which is read into memory and checked
against its SHA-256.
If \fIFILE\fR does not exist, \fBminerd\fR builds the data and saves it
there. Without this option it reads \fBverthash.dat\fR in the current
directory, or the one in Vertcoin Core's data directory, or else builds it
and saves it as \fBverthash.dat\fR.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Display version information and quit.
.TP
\fB\-x\fR, \fB\-\-proxy\fR=[\fISCHEME\fR://][\fIUSERNAME\fR:\fIPASSWORD\fR@]\fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR
Connect to the mining server through a proxy.
Supported schemes are: \fBhttp\fR, \fBsocks4\fR, \fBsocks5\fR.
Since libcurl 7.18.0, the following are also supported:
\fBsocks4a\fR, \fBsocks5h\fR (SOCKS5 with remote name resolving).
If no scheme is specified, the proxy is treated as an HTTP proxy.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\fBminerd\fR exits with status 0 when stopped by a signal (SIGINT or
SIGTERM), and 1 when it gives up: an error it cannot recover from, such as
a missing payout address, or \fB\-\-retries\fR failed attempts in a row.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The following environment variables can be specified in lower case or upper case;
the lower-case version has precedence. \fBhttp_proxy\fR is an exception
as it is only available in lower case.
.PP
.RS
.TP
\fBhttp_proxy\fR [\fISCHEME\fR://]\fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR
Sets the proxy server to use for HTTP.
.TP
\fBHTTPS_PROXY\fR [\fISCHEME\fR://]\fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR
Sets the proxy server to use for HTTPS.
.TP
\fBALL_PROXY\fR [\fISCHEME\fR://]\fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR
Sets the proxy server to use if no protocol-specific proxy is set.
.RE
.PP
Using an environment variable to set the proxy has the same effect as
using the \fB\-x\fR option.
.SH AUTHOR
Most of the code in minerd was written by
Pooler <pooler@litecoinpool.org> with contributions from others.
The multi-algorithm version was started by Lucas Jones, with algorithms
added by Tanguy Pruvot and others; see the AUTHORS file.

The original minerd was written by Jeff Garzik <jeff@garzik.org>.
